{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q005_cupredoxin_function_transfer","answer":"SCOPe groups these proteins into distinct families within the Cupredoxins superfamily (b.6.1), in the cupredoxin-like fold (b.6) of the all-beta class (b). Plastocyanin/azurin-like proteins constitute family b.6.1.1; ephrin ectodomains and the N-terminal domain of PAD4 belong to other families within that superfamily. Thus, SCOPe recognizes a probable distant evolutionary relationship despite their different biological roles. Superfamily membership supports common ancestry; sharing a fold alone would establish structural similarity without necessarily establishing homology.

Copper binding and electron transfer should not be transferred across these families. Canonical plastocyanins and azurins are type-1 copper electron carriers. Ephrins are Eph-receptor ligands involved in cell-surface signaling. PAD4 is a calcium-dependent peptidylarginine deiminase, but its catalytic machinery is in its C-terminal domain; neither that enzymatic activity nor copper-dependent electron transfer follows from its N-terminal cupredoxin classification.

A shared superfamily can support a structural comparison or a carefully evaluated domain template, but does not make one family a functional representative of all three. Copper-binding annotation requires evidence for the appropriate coordinating residues and three-dimensional site, ideally supported by metal-binding or spectroscopic measurements. Electron-transfer annotation additionally requires redox and biochemical evidence, including a relevant partner or physiological context.","evidence_used":["pretrained model knowledge"]}]}
